# 015 — Loading programs through the VFS instead of the bootloader RAMFS — done

- Type: feature · Priority: high · Status: done (2026-10-03)

## Problem
The bootloader read all programs into memory before ExitBootServices; the kernel could launch them only from those images.

## Done
- The UEFI bootloader passes the kernel only the service images (`BOOT_FILES`); there is no longer an application table in the ABI.
- The `loader` service (ring 3, with the spawn privilege) reads the ELF via `vfs_server` and calls `SPAWN_IMAGE` with a capability for its own buffer; the kernel parses and copies the image.
- Kernel shell: `RUN` and `LIST` are requests to the loader via a request page and a kernel message; the outcome is `LOADER_DONE`, with a wait of up to 15 s.
- Programs launch programs via `mind::process::spawn` (a CALL to the loader, a capability for the child's INIT slot).
- The task name is stored in the kernel (`ps`), not the image index; `dzenclk.elf` was renamed to `dzen-clock.elf`.

## Verification
- `services`: `LIST` shows the files on the disk; `hello` (a copy of clock.elf) and `extra/demo.elf`, which are not in any table, are launched; `kernel`, nonexistent and overly long names are rejected.
- `isolation`: `SPAWN_IMAGE` and `LOADER_DONE` are forbidden without the privilege.
- USB image: applications are loaded from a USB drive via xHCI.

## Audit (2026-10-04)

Done. `SPAWN_IMAGE` and the kernel-shell request page were replaced on purpose (020, 021).
